{
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // The `anneal` fork that closes a molecule-generalist trunk. One file for all
  // twelve cells — a fork is a separate submission per cell and is not part of
  // the chain, so this is run once per cell of `runs/molecule_generalist.jsonc`.
  //
  //   GPU=1 INDUCTOR_CACHE=.inductor_cache/generalist \
  //   src/generalist/tools/launch/run_cli.sh fork \
  //     --from src/generalist/results/runs/molecule_generalist_graph_s0/checkpoint-5599 \
  //     --mode anneal \
  //     --fork-config src/generalist/configs/forks/anneal_molecule_generalist.jsonc \
  //     --config src/generalist/configs/runs/molecule_generalist.jsonc \
  //     --cell molecule_generalist_graph_s0
  //
  // WHAT IT PRODUCES. The reportable model. The trunk runs WSD's stable phase at
  // a flat LR, so its last checkpoint is mid-schedule by construction and is not
  // the thing arm 1 should be differenced against. `min_factor` is left to the
  // run config's `lr_min`, which is `lr/10` (§7). At the default decay fraction
  // of 10 % this is 560 steps on a 5,599-step trunk.
  //
  // WHY `passes` IS RAISED, and why to 7. A corpus hands out `passes x
  // train_size` examples in total, and the trunk's budget rule spends the whole
  // allowance sizing its own step count, so a corpus that ends at its cap has
  // nothing left for an anneal appended to it. The fork's `passes` buys one more
  // pass of each, and one is enough on both horizons this file serves:
  //
  //   * The 5,599-step campaign (`runs/molecule_generalist.jsonc`). `mol/bbbp`
  //     ends at exactly its six-pass cap — that is what makes it the binding
  //     task (§2) — and no other corpus does. 560 decay steps at 56.83
  //     examples/step is 31,825 examples, of which BBBP's 2.35 % share is 746
  //     against the 1,244 one more pass buys.
  //   * The 11,140-step rerun (`probes/006_molecule_generalist_2x.jsonc`). Here
  //     **BACE, BBBP and ChEBI-20 all end at exactly 6.00 epochs**, because
  //     `budget_scale` thins a corpus to what it can sustain rather than stopping
  //     when the first one runs out — so three corpora need the bump where the
  //     campaign needed one. 1,114 decay steps at 57.13 examples/step is 63,644
  //     examples: ChEBI's 19.30 % share is 12,283 against 20,477, BBBP's 1.17 %
  //     is 745 against 1,244, BACE's 1.14 % is 726 against 1,208. Every one is
  //     inside a single extra pass, ChEBI with the least margin.
  //
  // The trunk's `passes` sizes the run and the fork's `passes` pays for the tail,
  // which is the same shape `anneal_cross_check.jsonc` uses.
  //
  // NOTE — g2s passes. `mol/g2s` is a *generator*, so its passes are files on
  // disk rather than a re-walk of one corpus, and `load` never generates — an
  // anneal that runs off the end of the built passes fails at the moment it is
  // wanted. The campaign needed 14 against its trunk's 12; the 2x rerun needs
  // ~27 against its trunk's 25, and both arms are built to 28
  // (`tools/campaign/build_g2s_tail.py`), so neither fork is blocked on a data build.
  // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "passes": {
    "mol/bbbp": 7, "mol/bace": 7, "mol/chebi20": 7,
    "mol/sider": 7, "mol/hiv": 7, "mol/tox21": 7
  },

  // 280, not the cross-check's 50. `eval_steps` scores the decay on its way down,
  // and when this was written a scoring firing was not cheap: the trunk's first
  // milestone cost 133 minutes on the flat arm, which is why it moved to
  // `milestone` (`config.py DEFAULT_VALIDATORS`). At 50 a 560-step decay would
  // fire eleven times and the anneal would cost more than the run it closes. 280
  // gives a midpoint and an endpoint, which is enough to see the decay behaving;
  // the number that matters is scored at the end of the leg regardless.
  //
  // That firing is now ~12 minutes on one card and ~4 on four
  // (`MOLECULE_GENERALIST.md` §6), so 50 would cost roughly two hours rather than
  // twenty-four and is affordable for the first time. Left at 280 anyway: what a
  // finer cadence buys is resolution on a curve that closes a leg of 560 steps,
  // and two points already show whether the decay is behaving. Lower it when
  // there is a question about the *shape* of the decay to answer.
  "eval_steps": 280
}
